2026-10-27|Construct a Grid With Exactly One Valid Path

Question:
Construct any m × n grid containing only '.' and '#', such that exactly one valid path exists from (0, 0) to (m - 1, n - 1), using only right and down moves.

Approach:
1. Create an m × n grid filled entirely with obstacles '#'.
2. Make every cell in the first row free by replacing it with '.'.
3. Make every cell in the last column free by replacing it with '.'.
4. The only valid path travels across the first row and then down the last column.
5. All other cells remain blocked, so no alternative path can reach the destination.
6. Return the constructed grid.

Solution:
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<char>> constructGrid(int m, int n) {
        vector<vector<char>> grid(m, vector<char>(n, '#'));

        // Make the first row free.
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            grid[0][j] = '.';
        }

        // Make the last column free.
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            grid[i][n - 1] = '.';
        }

        return grid;
    }
};

Example:

For m = 3 and n = 4, the constructed grid is:

```
